\begin_body
\begin_layout Title
Magnus quadrature formulas of order 10 on a centered interval
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Kenneth C. Johnson
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

ken@kjinnovation.com
\end_layout

\begin_layout Date
Version 02-Nov-2026
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Abstract
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\noindent
This paper extends the centered Magnus quadrature formulas of orders 2 to 8 in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "j28"
literal "false"
\end_inset
 to order 10 (residual error 
\begin_inset Formula $O(t^{11})$
\end_inset
 over the interval 
\begin_inset Formula $[-t,t]$
\end_inset
). The formula uses the centered Legendre moments 
\begin_inset Formula $g_0,\ldots,g_4$
\end_inset
 of the coefficient matrix and nine commutators. No formula with eight commutators was found. Of its 81 nonzero coefficients, 25 are small rationals and 56 are irrational algebraic numbers, defined as the isolated real solution of 70 polynomial equations with rational coefficients; they were refined by Newton's method to more than 190 digits, and the conditioning of the refinement is reported. The moments can be computed with five Gauss-Legendre nodes, with six Gauss-Lobatto nodes (five new samples per step), or with nine nodes that include the order-8 Newton-Cotes nodes. An embedded pair on seven Gauss-Kronrod nodes gives an order-6 estimate with three commutators and an order-10 estimate with seven more, whose difference serves as an error estimate.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Notation and order conditions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The notation follows 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "j28"
literal "false"
\end_inset
. The Magnus exponent 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega(t)$
\end_inset
 propagates the solution from 
\begin_inset Formula $-t$
\end_inset
 to 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset
, and the centered Legendre moments are
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
g_{k}(t)=\int_{-t}^{t}P_{k}(\tau/t)\cdot A(\tau)\,d\tau\label{eq:gdef}
\end{equation}

\end_inset
(Eq. 35 of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "j28"
literal "false"
\end_inset
). The moment 
\begin_inset Formula $g_k$
\end_inset
 is 
\begin_inset Formula $O(t^{k+1})$
\end_inset
 and has 
\emph on
weight
\emph default
 
\begin_inset Formula $k+1$
\end_inset
; 
\begin_inset Formula $g_0$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $g_2$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $g_4$
\end_inset
 are odd functions of 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $g_1$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $g_3$
\end_inset
 are even, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega(t)$
\end_inset
 contains only odd weights. The series of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset
 in the moments, derived from the centered Magnus theorem (Eq. 34 of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "j28"
literal "false"
\end_inset
) in exact rational arithmetic, is given through weight 7 in Eq. 41 of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "j28"
literal "false"
\end_inset
. Its weight-9 part, which involves only 
\begin_inset Formula $g_0,\ldots,g_4$
\end_inset
, has 45 nonzero terms in the Lyndon basis:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\Omega_{9}={}&-\tfrac{1}{2}[g_{3},g_{4}]+\tfrac{3}{28}[g_{0},[g_{2},g_{4}]]\\
&+\tfrac{7}{180}[[g_{0},g_{3}],g_{3}]-\tfrac{1}{28}[[g_{0},g_{4}],g_{2}]\\
&+\tfrac{9}{140}[g_{1},[g_{1},g_{4}]]-\tfrac{1}{4}[g_{1},[g_{2},g_{3}]]\\
&+\tfrac{1}{12}[[g_{1},g_{3}],g_{2}]-\tfrac{3}{280}[g_{0},[g_{0},[g_{1},g_{4}]]]\\
&-\tfrac{1}{72}[g_{0},[[g_{0},g_{3}],g_{2}]]+\tfrac{3}{280}[g_{0},[[g_{0},g_{4}],g_{1}]]\\
&+\tfrac{1}{280}[[g_{0},g_{1}],[g_{0},g_{4}]]-\tfrac{1}{30}[g_{0},[g_{1},[g_{1},g_{3}]]]\\
&-\tfrac{5}{168}[g_{0},[[g_{1},g_{2}],g_{2}]]-\tfrac{7}{120}[[g_{0},[g_{1},g_{3}]],g_{1}]\\
&-\tfrac{1}{72}[[g_{0},g_{2}],[g_{0},g_{3}]]+\tfrac{5}{168}[[g_{0},g_{2}],[g_{1},g_{2}]]\\
&-\tfrac{5}{168}[[[g_{0},g_{2}],g_{2}],g_{1}]+\tfrac{1}{40}[[[g_{0},g_{3}],g_{1}],g_{1}]\\
&-\tfrac{3}{56}[g_{1},[g_{1},[g_{1},g_{2}]]]+\tfrac{1}{1680}[g_{0},[g_{0},[g_{0},[g_{0},g_{4}]]]]\\
&-\tfrac{1}{720}[g_{0},[g_{0},[g_{0},[g_{1},g_{3}]]]]+\tfrac{1}{240}[g_{0},[g_{0},[[g_{0},g_{3}],g_{1}]]]\\
&+\tfrac{1}{240}[g_{0},[[g_{0},g_{1}],[g_{0},g_{3}]]]+\tfrac{1}{84}[g_{0},[g_{0},[g_{1},[g_{1},g_{2}]]]]\\
&+\tfrac{1}{84}[g_{0},[[g_{0},[g_{1},g_{2}]],g_{1}]]+\tfrac{5}{1008}[[g_{0},[g_{0},g_{2}]],[g_{0},g_{2}]]\\
&+\tfrac{1}{112}[g_{0},[[[g_{0},g_{2}],g_{1}],g_{1}]]-\tfrac{1}{180}[[g_{0},[g_{0},g_{3}]],[g_{0},g_{1}]]\\
&+\tfrac{1}{42}[[g_{0},g_{1}],[[g_{0},g_{2}],g_{1}]]+\tfrac{1}{56}[[[g_{0},g_{1}],g_{1}],[g_{0},g_{2}]]\\
&+\tfrac{3}{560}[[[[g_{0},g_{1}],g_{1}],g_{1}],g_{1}]+\tfrac{1}{4320}[g_{0},[g_{0},[g_{0},[g_{0},[g_{0},g_{3}]]]]]\\
&+\tfrac{1}{5040}[g_{0},[g_{0},[g_{0},[g_{0},[g_{1},g_{2}]]]]]+\tfrac{1}{1008}[g_{0},[g_{0},[g_{0},[[g_{0},g_{2}],g_{1}]]]]\\
&+\tfrac{1}{672}[g_{0},[g_{0},[[g_{0},g_{1}],[g_{0},g_{2}]]]]+\tfrac{1}{1120}[g_{0},[g_{0},[[[g_{0},g_{1}],g_{1}],g_{1}]]]\\
&-\tfrac{1}{672}[g_{0},[[g_{0},[g_{0},g_{2}]],[g_{0},g_{1}]]]-\tfrac{1}{504}[[g_{0},[g_{0},g_{1}]],[g_{0},[g_{0},g_{2}]]]\\
&-\tfrac{1}{1120}[g_{0},[[g_{0},g_{1}],[[g_{0},g_{1}],g_{1}]]]-\tfrac{1}{280}[[g_{0},[[g_{0},g_{1}],g_{1}]],[g_{0},g_{1}]]\\
&+\tfrac{1}{20160}[g_{0},[g_{0},[g_{0},[g_{0},[g_{0},[g_{0},g_{2}]]]]]]+\tfrac{1}{11200}[g_{0},[g_{0},[g_{0},[g_{0},[[g_{0},g_{1}],g_{1}]]]]]\\
&-\tfrac{1}{3360}[g_{0},[g_{0},[[g_{0},[g_{0},g_{1}]],[g_{0},g_{1}]]]]+\tfrac{1}{6720}[[g_{0},[g_{0},[g_{0},g_{1}]]],[g_{0},[g_{0},g_{1}]]]\\
&+\tfrac{1}{201600}[g_{0},[g_{0},[g_{0},[g_{0},[g_{0},[g_{0},[g_{0},g_{1}]]]]]]]
\end{aligned}\label{eq:series9}
\end{equation}

\end_inset
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A formula of order 10 must reproduce the series at weights 3, 5, 7 and 9. The free Lie algebra generated by 
\begin_inset Formula $g_0,\ldots,g_4$
\end_inset
 has 1, 5, 16 and 48 basis elements at these weights (not counting the single letters 
\begin_inset Formula $g_2$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $g_4$
\end_inset
, which occur in neither the series nor the formulas), so there are 70 
\emph on
order conditions
\emph default
 (Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:conditions"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
, which extends Table 2 of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "j28"
literal "false"
\end_inset
), compared with 0, 1, 6 and 20 at orders 2, 4, 6 and 8.
\end_layout

