\begin_layout Standard
\noindent
A generic rank below 70 does not by itself exclude eight commutators, because the nine-commutator solutions lie on a special set where the Jacobian rank drops to 55-56; the failure at eight commutators is strong numerical evidence, not a proof.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
The nine-commutator formula
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The operand parities of the chosen formula are, for 
\begin_inset Formula $C_1$
\end_inset
 to 
\begin_inset Formula $C_9$
\end_inset
, OE, OE, OO, OE, OO, OE, OO, OE and OE (O = odd, E = even); the commutators 
\begin_inset Formula $C_3$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $C_5$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $C_7$
\end_inset
 are even and enter only as operands. With 
\begin_inset Formula $C_i=[X_i,Y_i]$
\end_inset
 and coefficients 
\begin_inset Formula $x_1,\ldots,x_{81}$
\end_inset
 (Appendix A), of which 25 are rational and 56 irrational,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
X_{1}&=x_{1}g_{0}+x_{2}g_{2}\\
Y_{1}&=x_{3}g_{1}+x_{4}g_{3}\\
X_{2}&=x_{5}g_{0}+x_{6}g_{2}+x_{7}C_{1}\\
Y_{2}&=x_{8}g_{1}+x_{9}g_{3}\\
X_{3}&=x_{10}g_{0}+x_{11}g_{2}\\
Y_{3}&=x_{12}g_{0}+x_{13}g_{2}+x_{14}g_{4}+x_{15}C_{1}\\
X_{4}&=x_{16}g_{0}+x_{17}g_{2}+x_{18}g_{4}+x_{19}C_{2}\\
Y_{4}&=x_{20}g_{1}+x_{21}g_{3}+x_{22}C_{3}\\
X_{5}&=x_{23}g_{0}+x_{24}g_{2}+x_{25}g_{4}\\
Y_{5}&=x_{26}g_{0}+x_{27}g_{2}+x_{28}g_{4}+x_{29}C_{4}\\
X_{6}&=x_{30}g_{0}+x_{31}g_{2}+x_{32}g_{4}+x_{33}C_{2}+x_{34}C_{4}\\
Y_{6}&=x_{35}g_{1}+x_{36}g_{3}+x_{37}C_{3}+x_{38}C_{5}\\
X_{7}&=x_{39}g_{0}+x_{40}g_{2}+x_{41}g_{4}+x_{42}C_{2}+x_{43}C_{4}+x_{44}C_{6}\\
Y_{7}&=x_{45}g_{0}+x_{46}g_{2}+x_{47}g_{4}+x_{48}C_{1}+x_{49}C_{4}+x_{50}C_{6}\\
X_{8}&=x_{51}g_{0}+x_{52}g_{2}+x_{53}g_{4}+x_{54}C_{1}+x_{55}C_{2}+x_{56}C_{4}+x_{57}C_{6}\\
Y_{8}&=x_{58}g_{1}+x_{59}g_{3}+x_{60}C_{3}+x_{61}C_{5}+x_{62}C_{7}\\
X_{9}&=x_{63}g_{0}+x_{64}g_{2}+x_{65}g_{4}+x_{66}C_{1}+x_{67}C_{2}+x_{68}C_{4}+x_{69}C_{6}+x_{70}C_{8}\\
Y_{9}&=x_{71}g_{1}+x_{72}g_{3}+x_{73}C_{3}+x_{74}C_{5}+x_{75}C_{7}\\
\Omega(t)&=g_{0}+x_{76}C_{1}+x_{77}C_{2}+x_{78}C_{4}+x_{79}C_{6}+x_{80}C_{8}+x_{81}C_{9}+O(t^{11})
\end{aligned}\label{eq:f10}
\end{equation}

\end_inset
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The solution families have 16-20 free parameters beyond 24 rescaling freedoms. The member of Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:f10"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
 was chosen by minimizing the leading (
\begin_inset Formula $t^{11}$
\end_inset
) error coefficients, with each moment weighted by its leading size, and then fixing as many coefficients as possible to simple rationals while keeping that weighted error within 25% of the minimum; rescalings of the operands were then used to simplify the coefficients. Its weighted error is about 150 times smaller than for the member with the simplest coefficients, and the best points of the other families are within 6% of it on the test problems. Attempts to make all coefficients rational failed: in every order of fixing that was tried, some coefficients became determined at irrational values.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
The defining equations and root polishing
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
With the 25 rational coefficients substituted, the order conditions become a system of polynomial equations in the vector 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset
 of the 56 irrational coefficients. For each Lyndon word 
\begin_inset Formula $\ell$
\end_inset
 of odd weight 3 to 9 in the letters 
\begin_inset Formula $g_0,\ldots,g_4$
\end_inset
, the equation 
\begin_inset Formula $E_\ell(x)=0$
\end_inset
 states that the coefficient of the word 
\begin_inset Formula $\ell$
\end_inset
 in the expansion of the formula equals its coefficient in the series. Because a Lie polynomial is determined by its coefficients on the Lyndon words, the 70 equations are equivalent to the order conditions. They have rational coefficients, total degree at most 13 and 4951 terms in all; the weight-3 equation, for 
\begin_inset Formula $\ell=g_0g_1$
\end_inset
 and with 
\begin_inset Formula $x_1=5/3$
\end_inset
 substituted, is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\tfrac{1}{2} +\tfrac{5}{3}x_{3}x_{76} +x_{5}x_{8}x_{77} +x_{16}x_{20}x_{78} +x_{30}x_{35}x_{79} +x_{51}x_{58}x_{80} +x_{63}x_{71}x_{81}=0\label{eq:e1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\emph on
Root polishing.
\emph default
 The coefficients were refined by Gauss-Newton iteration (Newton's method for the overdetermined, consistent system), 
\begin_inset Formula $x\leftarrow x-(J^{T}J)^{-1}J^{T}E(x)$
\end_inset
, with 
\begin_inset Formula $E$
\end_inset
 and its Jacobian 
\begin_inset Formula $J$
\end_inset
 evaluated from the exact rational coefficients in 320-digit decimal arithmetic. Convergence is quadratic. The largest 
\begin_inset Formula $|E_\ell|$
\end_inset
 at each iteration, starting from the coefficients rounded to 17 and to 30 significant digits, is shown in Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:polish"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
.
\end_layout

