\begin_layout Standard
The final residual is at the level of the arithmetic precision, and the iterates agree with an earlier 150-digit solution to all of its digits. The values in Appendix A are rounded from this solution.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\emph on
Conditioning.
\emph default
 The Jacobian 
\begin_inset Formula $J=\partial E/\partial x$
\end_inset
 (
\begin_inset Formula $70\times56$
\end_inset
) has full column rank. Its singular values range from 11.5 to 
\begin_inset Formula $5.9\times10^{-8}$
\end_inset
, so its condition number is 
\begin_inset Formula $\kappa=2.0\times10^{8}$
\end_inset
; with each column scaled to unit norm, 
\begin_inset Formula $\kappa=2.3\times10^{7}$
\end_inset
. The two smallest singular values, 
\begin_inset Formula $5.9\times10^{-8}$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $3.6\times10^{-7}$
\end_inset
, lie well below the rest (the next is 
\begin_inset Formula $1.1\times10^{-5}$
\end_inset
). Full rank means that the solution is isolated, so every 
\begin_inset Formula $x_k$
\end_inset
 is a real algebraic number. It also bounds the error of the polished coefficients by the final residual divided by the smallest singular value, about 
\begin_inset Formula $10^{-310}$
\end_inset
 (the solution was stored to 200 digits). In the opposite direction, rounding the coefficients to double precision changes the residuals by at most about 
\begin_inset Formula $10^{-15}$
\end_inset
, far below the 
\begin_inset Formula $t^{11}$
\end_inset
 truncation error at any useful step size, so the condition number does not limit the accuracy of the formula in double-precision use. Integer-relation tests (LLL on 140-digit values of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2}$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{76}$
\end_inset
) excluded minimal polynomials of degree 12 or less with integer coefficients below about 
\begin_inset Formula $10^{10}$
\end_inset
, so closed forms, if they exist, are large.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Moment quadrature
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
By the criterion of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "j28"
literal "false"
\end_inset
 (moment-quadrature section), any rule of polynomial degree 9 gives moments accurate enough for order 10: 
\begin_inset Formula $g_0$
\end_inset
, which occurs alone, is then accurate to 
\begin_inset Formula $O(t^{11})$
\end_inset
, and 
\begin_inset Formula $g_k$
\end_inset
 for 
\begin_inset Formula $k\ge1$
\end_inset
 occurs only in terms of weight at least 
\begin_inset Formula $2k+1$
\end_inset
. Three such rules are given here. Each was verified by substituting its moments, expressed in the exact moments 
\begin_inset Formula $g_0,\ldots,g_9$
\end_inset
, into Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:f10"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
 and comparing every word through weight 10 with the series.
\end_layout

