\begin_layout Standard
\emph on
Nine nodes (modified Newton-Cotes).
\emph default
 With 
\begin_inset Formula $u_i=-1,-\frac{5}{6},-\frac{2}{3},-\frac{1}{3},0,\frac{1}{3},\frac{2}{3},\frac{5}{6},1$
\end_inset
, samples 
\begin_inset Formula $a_i=A(u_i\cdot t)$
\end_inset
, and weights obtained by integrating the interpolating polynomial against 
\begin_inset Formula $P_k$
\end_inset
,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
g_{0}&=\tfrac{t}{115500}\cdot(6235a_{0}+24576a_{1}+22440a_{2}+47025a_{3}+30448a_{4}\\
&\qquad+47025a_{5}+22440a_{6}+24576a_{7}+6235a_{8})\\
g_{1}&=\tfrac{t}{23100}\cdot(-1247a_{0}-4096a_{1}-2992a_{2}-3135a_{3}+3135a_{5}\\
&\qquad+2992a_{6}+4096a_{7}+1247a_{8})\\
g_{2}&=\tfrac{t}{1270500}\cdot(58295a_{0}+196608a_{1}-34320a_{2}-91575a_{3}-258016a_{4}\\
&\qquad-91575a_{5}-34320a_{6}+196608a_{7}+58295a_{8})\\
g_{3}&=\tfrac{t}{84700}\cdot(-3429a_{0}-8192a_{1}+9856a_{2}+11055a_{3}-11055a_{5}\\
&\qquad-9856a_{6}+8192a_{7}+3429a_{8})\\
g_{4}&=\tfrac{2t}{4129125}\cdot(69210a_{0}+80896a_{1}-282535a_{2}+1650a_{3}+261558a_{4}\\
&\qquad+1650a_{5}-282535a_{6}+80896a_{7}+69210a_{8})
\end{aligned}\label{eq:nine}
\end{equation}

\end_inset
The rule is exact for polynomials of degree 9 (degree 8 by interpolation, and degree 9 by symmetry). It needs eight new samples per step. Uniformly spaced nine-point Newton-Cotes nodes are not used, because of Runge's phenomenon: interpolation through equally spaced samples becomes unstable at high degree, and the nine-point Newton-Cotes weights alternate in sign, which amplifies rounding and data errors. (The 
\begin_inset Formula $g_0$
\end_inset
 weights above are all positive.) The nodes used here nevertheless lie on a uniform grid of spacing 
\begin_inset Formula $1/6$
\end_inset
 (
\begin_inset Formula $6u_i=0,\pm2,\pm4,\pm5,\pm6$
\end_inset
), and when a step is split into two half-steps, the nodes of the two halves include all nine nodes of the original step, as with conventional Newton-Cotes rules. The split then needs only eight new samples instead of sixteen. The nodes also contain the seven equally spaced nodes of the order-8 Newton-Cotes rule of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "j28"
literal "false"
\end_inset
, so an order-8 result is available from the same samples.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The leading quadrature error of 
\begin_inset Formula $g_0$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $\int_{-t}^{t}A(\tau)\,d\tau-(\text{rule})=\kappa\cdot t^{11}\cdot A^{(10)}(0)$
\end_inset
, has the coefficients in Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:kappa"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
, which extends Table 4 of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "j28"
literal "false"
\end_inset
; the Newton-Cotes column is for the nine-node rule. For the six-node Gauss-Lobatto rule 
\begin_inset Formula $\kappa=-1/1031443875$
\end_inset
, and the seven-node Gauss-Kronrod rule below is exact to degree 11, so its error enters only at 
\begin_inset Formula $O(t^{13})$
\end_inset
.
