\begin_layout Standard
Halving 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset
 reduces the order-10 errors by factors approaching 
\begin_inset Formula $2^{11}=2048$
\end_inset
 and the 
\begin_inset Formula $M_6$
\end_inset
 errors by factors approaching 
\begin_inset Formula $2^7=128$
\end_inset
. (In the rapidly varying test at 
\begin_inset Formula $t=0.05$
\end_inset
 the order-10 errors reach the rounding level of about 
\begin_inset Formula $2\times10^{-15}$
\end_inset
.) In the first two tests all moment rules give the same errors to within 1%. In the rapidly varying test the quadrature error of 
\begin_inset Formula $g_0$
\end_inset
 dominates: at 
\begin_inset Formula $t=0.1$
\end_inset
, exact or Kronrod moments are about 7 times more accurate than Gauss-Legendre moments, the nine-node rule is about 2 times more accurate, and the Lobatto rule is about 10% less accurate. The embedded 
\begin_inset Formula $M_{10}$
\end_inset
 is as accurate as Eq. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:f10"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
\end_inset
, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\|M_{10}-M_6\|$
\end_inset
 tracks the error of 
\begin_inset Formula $M_6$
\end_inset
 to two or three digits. Compared with order 8, the order-10 formula costs three more commutators and one more sample per step, and at 
\begin_inset Formula $t=0.1$
\end_inset
 it is 20-170 times more accurate in these tests.
\end_layout

